    "narrative": "The offensive engine for Ghost Ride the WHIP relies heavily on **James Wood** and **Brice Turang** today, who lead our roster with scores of 75.97 and 72.70, respectively. **James Wood** draws an exceptional matchup against a vulnerable lefty in Bratt, whose 5.90 xERA suggests a massive ceiling for production; his elite 0.426 xwOBA makes him an auto-start despite the minor SB-suppression warning. Similarly, **Brice Turang** benefits from a high-efficiency environment against Feltner, where his 19% boost from the opposing pitcher\u2019s skill level makes him our most reliable anchor. **Austin Riley** also warrants mention with a 72.42 score, buoyed by an aggressive +16% park factor adjustment that pushes him well into the \u201cStrong\u201d tier despite a suboptimal spot in the batting order.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involved **Isaac Paredes**, who landed on the bench with a 52.31 efficiency rating. While his 9% platoon advantage against Burke was attractive, he ultimately failed to clear the threshold for playing time compared to the likes of **Gabriel Moreno** and **Zach McKinstry**, whose positional flexibility and matchup profiles slightly outweighed the projected utility of **Isaac Paredes**. We remain cautious regarding **Seiya Suzuki**, who is staring down a brutal matchup against Skenes; his score of 45.36 reflects the severe penalty for facing such an elite arm. While we are currently fielding **Jose Caballero** for his speed upside, investors should monitor the catch-and-throw metrics, as the 1.86s pop time behind the plate for the opposition poses a tangible risk to our stolen base strategy.",
